From fdd3e998b137ae47e84b78921ce5a331c2d08c0c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 07:59:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 148/162] feat: colour wheels and contrast on the CineCam
 Grade, folded into one ASC CDL (S10 item 3a)

The Grade component grows the controls a colourist expects on a Primaries page -
Lift / Gamma / Gain with a master each, and Contrast about a Pivot - without the
renderer learning a single new thing. Every one of them folds into the ASC CDL
that already reaches the shader, exactly, on the CPU.

The mapping is the one Resolve, Nuke and every other lift/gamma/gain control
implements, and it is a mapping and not an approximation:

    slope = gain - lift        gain  = slope + offset
    offset = lift              lift  = offset
    power = 1 / gamma          gamma = 1 / power

so lift holds white put (x = 1 maps to 1 - lift + lift = 1) and gain holds black
put, which is what those controls do everywhere. The masters are the wheels' Y
rings: lift adds (neutral 0), gain and gamma multiply (neutral 1).

Contrast about a pivot is affine - c*(x - p) + p = c*x + p*(1 - c) - and the ASC
order is ONE affine step followed by ONE power. So contrast, the wheels and the
raw CDL all compose into a single (slope, offset) pair with one exponent:

    slope  = contrast * (gain - lift) * CDL_slope
    offset = contrast * ((gain - lift) * CDL_offset + lift) + pivot * (1 - contrast)
    power  = CDL_power / gamma

This is exact for EVERY value of power, not only power == 1, so the alternative
of adding two bus parameters and a few ALU inside the %_RT_SAMPLE0 block was not
needed - which is just as well, because all three .w slots of SceneCDLSlope /
Offset / Power are already spoken for (saturation, LMT grid, ODT grid). No new
bus parameter, no new shader constant, no renderer node. Pivot defaults to
0.4135878 = ACEScct(0.18), so contrast leaves a grey card exactly where it is.

Source of truth: the wheels and contrast compose ON TOP OF an ASC CDL base, and
the three base fields keep their old member ids. With the wheels neutral those
fields ARE what the camera publishes, number for number, which is what makes
pasting in a .cdl from Resolve exact. The derived numbers are never written back
into properties - the editor snapshots a component's properties for saving at
EntityObject.cpp:925, BEFORE it tells the component they changed at :930, so a
member written from an event handler renders but does not reliably save. They
are shown instead in the `grade published:` log line, which now prints the
folded numbers at four decimals and re-triggers on offset and power as well as
slope.

The one shader change is the CDL saturation's luma weights: the ASC CDL fixes
them at Rec.709 (0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722) regardless of the encoding the CDL is
applied in, and OCIO, Flame, Natron and Pomfort's on-set ACES CDL grading mode
in ACEScct all use exactly those. A number published as "ASC CDL saturation",
written into an EXR header and exported as a .cdl has to mean the same thing
here as in the application that reads the file. The AP1 argument at
CommonMath.cfi:245 is right for every other luminance in this pipeline and is
overruled only for this one, because this one is an interchange parameter. sat =
1 remains an exact identity, so nothing moves for an ungraded picture, and the
stock path never reaches the line.

TrackView was verified in 5.7.1's source rather than assumed:
CAnimEntityNode::UpdateDynamicParams_Editor walks the entity's components and
serialises every reflected member (CryMovie/EntityNode.cpp:446-491), reached by
right-clicking the entity node -> Add Track -> Components -> CineCam Grade ->
Grade -> the property. bool, float, Vec3 and Schematyc::Range<> all produce
tracks; ColorF also does but keys in 0-255 with alpha clobbered
(EntityNode.cpp:326/334), which is why the wheels are Vec3 and not colour
pickers - a gain above 1 or a negative lift could not survive that. The
persisted track key is the member NAME, so the new names are frozen from here.
The component is notified through the ENTITY_EVENT_COMPONENT_PROPERTY_CHANGED it
already handles, on the frames a value really moves (EntityNode.cpp:2843-2848),
so an animated grade needs no plumbing - but ReportStatus() is now on a
per-frame path and gained a cheap integer early-out before any string work.

SCineGradeParams::operator== is field-wise instead of memcmp: the struct has
padding after the bool and padding bytes are not guaranteed equal between two
structs holding the same values.

Neutral is still an exact identity end to end: no grade component, an untouched
grade component, and every neutral typed by hand are the same picture - pure
ACES 2.0. Stock path bit-identical.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s10-grade-component.md section 3a.
Research (written first): scene-notes/research/s10-3a-wheels-cdl-trackview.md.

