+*Scene-referred path only. Everything in this group is a way of writing ONE ASC CDL - the
+interchange every grading tool speaks - applied in ACEScct to scene data BEFORE the output
+transform. All of it is live: a dragged slider moves the picture on the next frame, and every
+property is animatable in TrackView.*
+
+Three colour wheels in the convention Resolve, Nuke and every other lift/gamma/gain control share,
+plus contrast about a pivot:
 
 | Property | Range | Default | Effect |
 | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 | Bypass Grade | bool | false | Take white balance, CDL and the look out of the chain in one click and leave only the output transform. The A/B - what the shot looks like ungraded, with the exposure and the optics unchanged. **false = the grade is live.** |
-| Slope (RGB) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | ASC CDL slope: a multiply in log - gain, the highlight end. **1 = neutral.** Applied first: `out = (in * slope + offset) ^ power`. |
-| Offset (RGB) | float3 | (0, 0, 0) | ASC CDL offset: an add in log - lift, the shadow end, and the usual place a colour cast is corrected. **0 = neutral.** |
-| Power (RGB) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | ASC CDL power: gamma, the midtones, applied last. **1 = neutral**; below 1 opens the mids, above 1 closes them. |
-| Saturation | 0-4 (slider 0-2) | 1 | The ASC saturation extension, after the SOP triple, about the luminance of the graded value with AP1 weights. **1 = neutral**, 0 = monochrome. Because it happens in the working space and before the output transform, pushing it does not drift the hues the way a saturation slider on a finished picture does. |
-
-The order is the ASC specification's and is not negotiable if the numbers are to round-trip with
-Resolve: slope, then offset, then power on the clamped result, per channel with no cross-channel
-term; saturation is the ASC's own extension and comes after.
+| Lift (RGB) | float3 | (0, 0, 0) | The shadow end. Lifting the blacks leaves **white exactly where it was**; negative crushes. The place a colour cast in the shadows is corrected. **0 = neutral.** |
+| Lift Master | -1 to 1 | 0 | The wheel's Y ring: added to all three Lift channels at once. **0 = neutral.** |
+| Gamma (RGB) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | The midtones - the CDL power read the colourist's way round, so **above 1 opens** the mids and below 1 closes them. Neither black nor white moves. **1 = neutral.** |
+| Gamma Master | 0-4 (slider 0-2) | 1 | Multiplies all three Gamma channels at once. **1 = neutral.** |
+| Gain (RGB) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | The highlight end, a multiply in log. Raising it leaves **black exactly where it was**. With Lift at 0 this is the ASC slope itself. **1 = neutral.** |
+| Gain Master | 0-4 (slider 0-2) | 1 | Multiplies all three Gain channels at once. **1 = neutral.** |
+| Contrast | 0-4 (slider 0-2) | 1 | Steepen or flatten the whole curve about the pivot. **1 = neutral**, 0 = a flat grey field. |
+| Contrast Pivot | 0-1 | 0.4136 | The code value Contrast turns about, in ACEScct. The default is ACEScct(0.18), i.e. **18 % grey stays put** while everything around it opens or closes. Lower it to pivot on the shadows, raise it for the highlights. |
+| Saturation | 0-4 (slider 0-2) | 1 | The ASC saturation extension, after the SOP triple, about the luminance of the graded value **with the ASC's Rec.709 weights** - so the number means here exactly what it means in Resolve, OCIO or Flame. **1 = neutral**, 0 = monochrome. Because it happens in the working space and before the output transform, pushing it does not drift the hues the way a saturation slider on a finished picture does. |
+
+And the raw ASC CDL the wheels sit on top of - the place to paste a `.cdl` that came from Resolve:
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| CDL Slope (base) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | ASC CDL slope. **1 = neutral.** ASC order: `out = (in * slope + offset) ^ power`. |
+| CDL Offset (base) | float3 | (0, 0, 0) | ASC CDL offset - the raw one, which moves white as well as black. (The Lift wheel is the one that holds white put.) **0 = neutral.** |
+| CDL Power (base) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | ASC CDL power. **1 = neutral**; below 1 opens the mids, above 1 closes them - the opposite sense to the Gamma wheel, which is `1 / this`. |
+
+**How the two halves combine, and why it matters.** The wheels, contrast and the CDL base all fold
+into a **single** ASC CDL before anything is published:
+
+```
+slope  = contrast * (gain - lift) * CDL_slope
+offset = contrast * ((gain - lift) * CDL_offset + lift) + pivot * (1 - contrast)
+power  = CDL_power / gamma
+```
+
+per channel, with the masters folded into `lift`, `gain` and `gamma` first (lift adds, gain and
+gamma multiply). The fold is **exact** - it works out that way because the ASC order is one affine
+step followed by one power, and lift, gain, contrast and the CDL base are all affine. Two
+consequences worth having:
+
+* **with the wheels and contrast at their neutrals, the three CDL fields ARE what the camera
+  publishes**, number for number, so pasting in a `.cdl` from Resolve reproduces it exactly;
+* **there is no picture this group can make that a `.cdl` cannot carry**, which is what makes the
+  Resolve round trip (`docs/SceneReferredExport.md`) a real test rather than an approximation.
+
+The folded numbers - the ones actually on the bus - are printed by the camera whenever they change
+(`[CinematicCamera] grade published: slope ... offset ... power ... saturation ...`), written into
+every exported EXR as `rec/cdl`, and written beside a capture as an ASC `.cdl` sidecar. They are
+deliberately **not** written back into the properties: the editor snapshots a component's
+properties for saving *before* it tells the component they changed, so a value written from an
+event handler would render but not reliably save.
 
